@metadata
title = Command line
summary = Every command, every flag, and what the exit codes mean.
@endmetadata

# Command line

@code(language="shell")
xtxt <command> <file>... [options]
@endcode

Use `-` as the filename to read from standard input.

## Commands

@table
Command | What it does
--------|-------------
`validate` | Check syntax and report problems
`lint` | Validate, plus style warnings
`render` | Render to the terminal
`export` | Convert to another format
`import` | Convert Markdown to XTXT
`ast` | Print the parse tree as JSON
`extract` | Print the machine-facing view as JSON
`paste` | Append the clipboard image to a document
@endtable

## validate and lint

@code(language="shell")
xtxt validate notes.xtxt
xtxt lint notes.xtxt
xtxt validate *.xtxt
@endcode

`validate` reports errors and warnings. `lint` adds style warnings on top —
things that parse and mean what you wanted but are worth reconsidering.

Exit status is non-zero only when there is an **error**. Warnings never fail a
build, because an unknown directive is a warning and a document from the future
must not break a pipeline built today.

## render

@code(language="shell")
xtxt render notes.xtxt
xtxt render notes.xtxt -w 100
xtxt render notes.xtxt --no-color
@endcode

## export

@code(language="shell")
xtxt export notes.xtxt html -o notes.html
xtxt export notes.xtxt md
xtxt export notes.xtxt text -w 72
@endcode

@table
Format | Output
-------|-------
`html` | Standalone document with styling
`body` | HTML fragment, no `<head>`, for embedding
`md` | CommonMark
`text` | Plain text, wrapped
`json` | Parse tree
@endtable

## import

@code(language="shell")
xtxt import notes.md -o notes.xtxt
@endcode

Converts CommonMark to XTXT: headings, emphasis, lists, links, tables and code
fences all map across. Tables become `@table`, fenced code becomes `@code` with
its language preserved.

## ast and extract

@code(language="shell")
xtxt ast notes.xtxt          # the parse tree
xtxt extract notes.xtxt      # the machine-facing view
@endcode

`ast` is the tree every implementation must produce, and what the conformance
suite pins. `extract` is the interpreted view: outline, tasks, records, links,
media and code, each with a line number.

## paste

@code(language="shell")
xtxt paste notes.xtxt
xtxt paste notes.xtxt --embed
xtxt paste notes.xtxt --caption "Whiteboard" --width 600
@endcode

Reads an image from the system clipboard, writes it beside the document and
appends the `@image` directive. Uses whatever the platform provides —
`osascript` on macOS, PowerShell on Windows, `wl-paste` or `xclip` on Linux.

## Options

@table
Flag | Effect
-----|-------
`--resolve` | Expand `@include` and `@embed` first
`--interactive` | In HTML, inline the chart runtime; without it, output has no script
`--mermaid` | In HTML, load the diagram renderer from a CDN
`--plugins <path>` | Load a plugin manifest for unknown directives
`-o <path>` | Write to a file instead of stdout
`-w <n>` | Wrap column for text output, default 80
`--no-color` | Disable ANSI colour
@endtable

## Diagnostics

@code(language="text")
notes.xtxt:14: error: unclosed @code block: no matching @endcode
notes.xtxt:22: warning: unknown directive @youtube (preserved, but this reader cannot render it)
@endcode

@table
Level | Meaning
------|--------
error | The file cannot be interpreted unambiguously
warning | It parses, but something is suspect
@endtable

## In CI

@code(language="shell")
xtxt validate docs/**/*.xtxt || exit 1
@endcode

Because warnings do not affect exit status, this fails only on genuine
breakage. To be stricter, read the JSON and decide for yourself:

@code(language="shell")
xtxt ast notes.xtxt | jq -e '.issues | length == 0'
@endcode
